# Line-ending and binary policy.
#
# Why this file exists: `scripts/build.mjs` writes the committed `api/`
# bundles with LF, because esbuild always emits LF. With no rule pinning
# them, a Windows clone running the default `core.autocrlf=true` wants those
# ten files to be CRLF in the working tree — so `git status` reported all ten
# as modified from the moment anyone ran `npm run build`, while `git diff`
# showed no content change at all, because the bytes were byte-for-byte
# identical to HEAD. A permanently dirty working tree gets ignored for the
# same reason a permanently red CI check does, and it hides the real thing
# `.github/workflows/frontend.yml` is watching for: bundles that have
# genuinely drifted from their sources.
#
# After adding rules here, run `git add --renormalize .` once.

# Baseline. Text files are stored LF in the repository; Git picks the
# working-tree ending per platform unless a rule below pins it. Binary files
# are detected and left untouched.
* text=auto

# ── Generated, committed, and required to match a fresh build byte for byte ──
# `api/` is deliberately tracked (see .gitignore for why Vercel needs it in
# the clone), and CI rebuilds it and fails on any diff. Pinning the working
# tree to LF is what makes that check mean the same thing on a Windows
# machine as it does on ubuntu-latest.
api/**/*.js       text eol=lf

# ── Consumed by tools that require LF ──
# A CRLF shebang makes the kernel search for an interpreter literally named
# "/usr/bin/env bash\r"; scripts/migrate.sh is run against production.
*.sh              text eol=lf
# psql feeds these into dollar-quoted function bodies, where a stray CR
# becomes part of the SQL text rather than whitespace.
*.sql             text eol=lf

# ── Binary: never inspect, never convert ──
*.png             binary
*.webp            binary
*.zip             binary
